# RDMA on OpenShift SR-IOV

Scope: what RDMA is, when it is needed, and how to enable it on SR-IOV virtual functions using the OpenShift SR-IOV Network Operator. Hardware reference: Intel E810 (vendor `8086`, device `1593`, driver `ice`), 25 GbE, OpenShift 4.14 / 4.18.

---

## 1. What is RDMA

RDMA (Remote Direct Memory Access) is a data transfer method where the network adapter reads from and writes to application memory directly, on both the sending and receiving host, without involving the operating system kernel in the data path.

### 1.1 Normal network path vs RDMA path

| Step | Normal socket path (TCP/IP) | RDMA path |
|---|---|---|
| Application sends data | `send()` on a socket | Posts a work request pointing at a registered memory buffer |
| Kernel involvement | Copies data into kernel buffers, runs TCP/IP, raises interrupts | None – kernel is bypassed |
| CPU cost per byte | High – scales with throughput | Near zero – the NIC does the work |
| Latency | Tens of microseconds | Low single-digit microseconds |
| Receiving side | Kernel receives, copies into application buffer | NIC writes straight into the remote application buffer |

The result is very low latency, high throughput and almost no CPU consumption for data movement. The price is that both ends must be RDMA-capable, the memory must be pinned, and (on Ethernet) the fabric must be configured to be lossless.

### 1.2 RDMA transports

| Transport | Medium | Notes |
|---|---|---|
| InfiniBand | Dedicated IB fabric | Native RDMA, needs IB switches and a subnet manager |
| RoCEv2 | Standard Ethernet, UDP port 4791 | Most common in data centres; **this is what the E810 uses** |
| iWARP | Ethernet, runs over TCP | Older, less common |

On the E810, RDMA is provided by the `irdma` kernel driver running on top of the `ice` (PF) and `iavf` (VF) drivers.

### 1.3 How RDMA fits on an SR-IOV VF

Enabling RDMA does not replace the normal network interface on a VF. It adds a second device on top of the same VF:

```
VF 9 on ens1f2 (deviceType: netdevice)
 ├── iavf driver  -> ens1f2v9        (normal IP interface, TCP/UDP apps)
 └── irdma driver -> rocep17s0f2v9   (RDMA verbs device, only with isRdma: true)
```

Applications that use sockets keep using the `iavf` interface. Applications that use RDMA verbs open the `irdma` device.

