## 2. When RDMA is required

Enable RDMA **only** when a workload actually uses it. An RDMA application can be recognised by:

- It links against `libibverbs` / `librdmacm` (or `libfabric` / UCX built on them)
- It requires `/dev/infiniband/*` devices inside the pod
- Its documentation mentions RoCE, InfiniBand, "verbs", or GPUDirect

### 2.1 Typical RDMA consumers

| Area | Examples |
|---|---|
| AI / GPU training | NCCL, RCCL, oneCCL, PyTorch/TensorFlow multi-node, DeepSpeed, vLLM sharding |
| HPC | Open MPI, MPICH, Intel MPI, MVAPICH2; WRF, OpenFOAM, GROMACS, LAMMPS |
| Storage | NVMe-oF/RDMA, iSER, SMB Direct, Lustre, GPFS, BeeGFS, WekaFS, Ceph RDMA messenger |
| Databases | Oracle RAC / Exadata, SAP HANA scale-out, SQL Server over SMB Direct |
| Virtualisation | VMware vSAN / vMotion RDMA, Storage Spaces Direct |

### 2.2 Workloads that do NOT need RDMA

| Workload type | Why RDMA is not needed |
|---|---|
| DPDK user-plane pods (`deviceType: vfio-pci`) | The application owns the whole VF in userspace; neither `iavf` nor `irdma` is loaded. `isRdma` does not apply and is rejected by the operator webhook. |
| Ordinary kernel networking pods (OAM, signalling, HTTP, gRPC) | They use sockets, which go through `iavf`. An RDMA device would be present but never opened. |

In the current cluster configuration (BPP = `vfio-pci`, BIP = `netdevice`) every policy correctly has `isRdma: false`. The configuration below is for onboarding a future RDMA consumer.

