## 7. Removal

To remove RDMA capability without touching the other VF groups:

```bash
oc delete sriovnetwork rdma-net -n openshift-sriov-network-operator
oc delete sriovnetworknodepolicy policy-ens1f2-rdma -n openshift-sriov-network-operator
```

VFs 9–12 return to the default `iavf` binding with no RDMA device; `numVfs` on `ens1f2` stays at 16, so no node reboot is expected.

---

## 8. Summary

- RDMA = NIC moves data directly between application memories, bypassing the kernel; on E810 this is RoCEv2 via the `irdma` driver.
- Enable it per VF group with `deviceType: netdevice` + `isRdma: true`; the VF keeps its normal `iavf` interface and gains an RDMA device.
- The switch fabric must be lossless (PFC + ECN) and UDP 4791 must be open.
- Pods need `IPC_LOCK`, the RDMA resource request, and an image with `rdma-core`.
- Only enable RDMA for workloads that actually use verbs (AI/GPU, HPC/MPI, NVMe-oF, RDMA storage). DPDK (`vfio-pci`) and plain socket workloads do not need it.
